    "topic": "Bell wanted us to answer the phone with ahoy",
    "summary": "Alexander Graham Bell answered the telephone with 'ahoy' ('ahoy-hoy'), but there is no evidence the greeting caught on. Thomas Edison, in a letter of 15 August 1877 to T. B. A. David, wrote that 'Hello!' can be heard 10 to 20 feet away, and is credited with popularising hello on the telephone. By 1889 telephone operators were known as 'hello-girls'. The word itself is older: 'hello' is in print by 1826 and 'hullo' by 1803.",
    "angle": "The phone greeting that lost.",
